members of the Trump administration will  go to Capitol Hill today to testify  about the president's proposed budget  it faces pushback from Democrats and  some Republicans Congress ultimately  sets the budget some lawmakers are  concerned about more than eight hundred  billion dollars in proposed cuts over  the next decade to Medicaid food  assistance and health insurance for  low-income children budget director Mick  Mulvaney said the reductions will help  make sure taxpayer money is not wasted  we are not kicking anybody off of any  program who really needs it that's not  we have plenty of money in this country  to take care of the people who need help  okay and we will do that we don't have  enough money to take care of people  everybody who doesn't need help  the president's proposal does protect  Social Security and Medicare  how Governor John Kasich has experienced  dealing with the nation's budget as a  congressman he chaired the House Budget  Committee he oversaw the 1995 government  shutdown and a balanced federal budget  the governor is the author of the book  two paths America divided our United  Governor Kasich welcome back thanks  charlie what impact will this budget as  presented have on the state of Ohio  would it be significant Charlie but this  isn't going to pass you know presidents  end up budgets and then the Congress  fiddles around to things to keep in mind  number one we have a program in Ohio  where we're saying to people we will  help you but then we need you to assume  personal responsibility we'll help you  to overcome your problems the problem  here is that you can't just take stuff  away when people need to be healthy they  need to be fed you know so it's not just  a one-way street it has to be both ways  so the other thing is the Congress will  play around with this thing we don't  know where end up but here's a problem  with debt if they don't come to grips  with debt its rising higher and higher  as a percentage of our economy when that  goes up job opportunities go down and  when debt comes down job opportunities  go up that's why it's important we  restructure the federal government  governor what do you say to those who  say a society and a civilization can be  measured by how well it treats its  weakest members I would agree with that  and so I think that the changes the  reforms that can come from any of these  programs are fine but you just can't  pull  rug out from under people if they're not  healthy they're not going to work and if  they're not huh if they're hungry  they're not going to go to work so you  have to take care of both of those  things at the same time you're trying to  bring about reform about the number that  he's listing for a drug control 27  billion dollars the big story for most  states now is the opioid epidemic your  state has been greatly affected what do  you make of that number how do you think  the money should be spent on that we  have to spend some money on this  obviously we spend almost a billion  dollars and we've been on this for seven  years and trying to fight this but let  me tell you the bottom line I met with  the drug enforcement agency officials 28  30 32 years they said what do we do let  me tell you it starts in kindergarten  our schools have to be the frontline  mothers and fathers all these you know  mothers and fathers or civic  organizations but the school starting in  kindergarten all the way through the  12th grade have to have a consistent and  constant message to kids stay off the  drugs it'll either kill you you'll end  up in jail and you will lose the purpose  for which the Lord's created you and it  is critical that the schools are the  frontline and engaged in this and  sometimes it's difficult to get them  involved but it is essential if we are  going to win this battle  kindergarten seems so young you're 5  years old you know what six seven years  old you know we can skip with 5 but  there's ways to talk to kids about how  dangerous these things are the same way  we talk to them about the dangers of so  many other things  drugs is it's destroying you know sort  of the culture of our country and we can  we can win this these budgets reflect  the values of a president every every  year and of course as you mentioned  where the budget director yeah right  even as you mentioned Congress Congress  changes things but the budget director  said yesterday this budget we look at  this through the eyes of the people who  are actually paying the bills yeah the  people who pay taxes is that a way to do  a budget no I think you when you do  budgets and look I I went through I was  the chairman of the committee when we  balanced the federal budget the economy  got better but it's a matter of creating  priorities reform is fine privatization  is fine but you cannot just move quickly  to pull the the rug under from people  who need help but at the same time Nora  we have to make sure that we get to the  root of their problems so where does  Congress fall short first of all they  don't permit people to get  Didi's and in as a substitute to the  work requirements you have people don't  know in America you know if you're on  welfare you have to work 30 hours a week  if you get food stamps I actually wrote  this you have to work 20 hours a week  but let's make that work be constructive  so that they can have a future so that  they can have skills so they can get a  job that's what it's all about and the  job training programs offered by the  federal government they're frankly their  I'll say they're pretty much a joke when  it comes to giving people giving States  the flexibility to do what we need to do  to train our people so they have the  skills to work can we talk about the  Manchester attack for a second your  state is known for hosting big events  for instance the Republic I was just at  a rock concert on Friday night with  about 30,000 people a concert just  curious well I went to see the group the  band live which and it was right the  inside garden was supposed to be on  right after that and by the way The Lost  Chris Cornell because of drugs but are  you looking are you looking at different  safety protocols now in light of this  attack whenever we have a concert like  that the Highway Patrol all the security  is out there you know I have two  seventeen year old daughters and they're  very interested in music and got this  just I watched your your coverage this  morning it just broke my heart you know  sometimes we read it in a newspaper  whatever but you see the mothers that  are crying and they don't know where  their children are and are they going to  they know that they're not coming back  but they're holding out hope that  they're in a hospital somewhere look we  can't run away and hi but we have to do  everything that we can do to correct not  protect not just the inside but a little  bit of the perimeter but what I think is  so important is who helped this guy  build this vest we have to track this  down and it means good intelligence and  it means cooperation worldwide and it  also means and I give President Trump  credit for this everybody in this world  is civilized whether you're Christian  Jew Muslim doesn't matter we all have to  stand up and make the argument that this  culture of death of destroying young  people is not acceptable and we must  stop it out and those who sit on the  fence to think about this we have to  pull them over and say you cannot be  somebody that brings tear  to people of all ages in all race and  all gender right Governor Kasich I know  most people agree with that sentiment  thank you very much yes thank you at the  table always good to have you here god  bless them

